Fixed in `tokio-stream/src/stream_map.rs:686`:

```rust
fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
    let mut ret: (usize, Option<usize>) = (0, Some(0));

    for (_, stream) in &self.entries {
        let hint = stream.size_hint();

        ret.0 = ret.0.saturating_add(hint.0);

        match (ret.1, hint.1) {
            // The upper bound is only known if it does not overflow.
            (Some(a), Some(b)) => ret.1 = a.checked_add(b),
            (Some(_), None) => ret.1 = None,
            _ => {}
        }
    }

    ret
}
```

- Lower bound saturates at `usize::MAX` — a lower bound is always a valid claim if it's smaller than the true one.
- Upper bound becomes `None` when the sum overflows, i.e. "no known upper bound", which is the only honest answer since the real total isn't representable.
- The explicit `(usize, Option<usize>)` annotation is needed because `(0, Some(0))` alone leaves the integer type ambiguous once the methods are called on it.

This matches the existing convention in the crate — `merge_size_hints` in `tokio-stream/src/stream_ext.rs:1216` already uses `saturating_add`/`checked_add` the same way, so `Merge` was never affected.

Tests: `size_hint_overflow` now passes, and the full `tokio-stream` suite is green (28 passed in `stream_stream_map`, no failures across the crate). `cargo clippy` isn't installed in this environment, so that check wasn't run.
